    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d192">

                <pb facs="0235" n="1069" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1069"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/10338</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in France</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Sharp</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-10-17T17:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 17, 1918, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5937. For McFadden [from War Trade Board]:</p>
                <p>No. 383. Your 437 [<hi rendition="#italic">457</hi>], Embassy 5235, September 30,
                    midnight; and your 485, Embassy 5350, October 1 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >7</hi>], 11 p.m.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d192-f1"/> The Government of the United
                    States has not yet been approached by the Spanish Government on the subject of
                    trading-with-enemy list for Spain. Consequently, we cannot participate in any
                    joint answer which may be sent by the British and French. If, however, the
                    British and French propose making a more definite answer than that referred to
                    your No. 437 [<hi rendition="#italic">457</hi>], we would be glad to assist in
                    presenting a unified front to the Spanish Government by agreeing that in their
                    note they may state that the Government of the United States has been advised of
                    their views as therein expressed and concurs therein. If this were done, we
                    should, of course, desire first to study the form of note which the British and
                    French would propose to deliver.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
